WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.163
Two open questions drive the paper.

00:00:04.243 --> 00:00:08.406
First,

00:00:08.486 --> 00:00:12.649
can a VQA model generate a human-readable
explanation while still maintaining its answer accuracy?

00:00:12.729 --> 00:00:16.892
Second, how good are those generated explanations,

00:00:16.972 --> 00:00:21.135
and how should we even evaluate them?

00:00:21.215 --> 00:00:25.378
Existing explainable-VQA datasets suggest using
conventional natural language metrics such as BLEU and ROUGE,

00:00:25.458 --> 00:00:29.621
but these were designed for string matching over
overlapping n-grams,

00:00:29.701 --> 00:00:33.864
not for judging whether an explanation truly
supports an answer.

00:00:33.944 --> 00:00:38.107
The authors argue that as reasoning problems in VQA
grow more complex,

00:00:38.187 --> 00:00:42.350
having an interpretable, well-evaluated explanation
is no longer optional but urgent.
